color-theme: biotechnology

=== layout: section

# Fund a result you can evaluate

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Four numbered project goals](assets/business_plan_samples/virona_milestones.png)

@caption

Which result can you measure? *Virona excerpt.*

@notes

Original instructor emphasis: "Again I will look for feasible milestones."
Borrow the numbered progression. Then ask how success would be measured, what the control is, and what would stop the project.
The source words "safe," "non-toxic," and the proposed clinical transition require evidence; the screenshot is not a validated development sequence.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: two-panels

# A milestone ends with evidence

@left

## Activity

Research the product.

Raise money.

Start selling.

@right

## Milestone

Compare performance with a defined control under stated conditions.

Use that result to decide whether to proceed, revise, or stop.

@notes

This is a library-research project. Students propose justified development tests and
criteria; they are not required to conduct laboratory experiments for this assignment.

=== layout: one-panel

# Build a sequence of decisions

@body

| Stage | Evidence needed | Decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanism | A result in the relevant model | Does the proposed action occur? |
| Prototype | Repeatable performance against a comparator | Is the product useful? |
| Practical use | Delivery, production, and user constraints | Can it work in the intended setting? |
| Expansion | Evidence from the initial use case | Is wider use justified? |

@notes

Adapt the stages to the technology. Virona's sample makes successive development steps
visible, but its proposed outcomes and timelines still need independent support.
Do not assume that success in one model guarantees success in the next setting.

=== layout: one-panel

# Put the largest uncertainty on the page

@body

| Uncertainty | What the plan should explain |
| --- | --- |
| Scientific | The result needed to support the mechanism |
| Practical | Manufacturing, stability, delivery, and scale |
| Commercial | Who buys and what makes them switch |
| Responsible use | Risks, containment, and required oversight |

@notes

Aquazyme motivates the distinction between a controlled process and environmental release.
Therapeutic proposals motivate checking safety, delivery, and relevant regulatory sources.
Research the requirements for the actual product and jurisdiction; do not assume approval.

=== layout: two-panels

# Management means responsibility

@left

## Connect a person to a task

- Scientific mechanism and evidence
- Market and competition research
- Cost model and milestones
- Writing, figures, and final review

@right

## Identify missing expertise

- Which milestone needs outside help?
- What skill would an adviser provide?
- Which proposed partners still need agreement?

@notes

Describe actual team experience.
Do not invent degrees, partnerships, employees, or a commitment from a real organization.
For the course project, at least two members review each major section.

=== layout: one-panel

# Tie the funding request to milestones

@body

| Milestone | Cost categories | Funding buys |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanism test | Staff time, materials, analysis | Evidence for the core claim |
| Prototype | Development, quality checks, iteration | A reproducible product candidate |
| Practical-use study | Production, delivery, evaluation | Evidence for the intended setting |

@notes

Add justified estimates and timing in the full plan. Include projected revenue and
expenses over the funded period, their assumptions, and a contingency for delays.
Distinguish funding already received from funding proposed or being sought.
Avoid treating a hoped-for grant, partner, sale, or exit as an accomplished fact.

=== layout: two-panels

# Describe an advantage you can support

@left

## Possible sources of advantage

- Better performance on a defined measure
- Easier fit with a customer's workflow
- Lower total cost under stated assumptions
- A defensible method or capability

@right

## Evidence to provide

- A fair comparison
- Sources for existing technology
- A realistic development plan
- Clear status of intellectual-property claims

@notes

Do not label the company patent-pending or protected merely because the idea is new to you.
Patentability and permission to commercialize are research questions, not promises here.
